      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0",
      "artist": "NealVickers",
      "description": "Capitol Reef National Park in south-central Utah is a region of cliffs, canyons, domes and bridges in the Waterpocket Fold, a wrinkle on the earth that runs almost 100 miles.  The name \"capitol\" came from the resemblance of the white domes of Navajo Sandstone that looked like capitol building domes.  \"Reef\" was derived from the rocky cliffs.  These geologic formations were created by nearly 300 million years of geologic deposits, uplifts, and erosion.  The sediments of the cliffs can look similar to a layer cake.",
